[00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:31.860]
But just a bit more about whisper.

[00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:35.960]
It's 50 bits of information packaged into 162 bit message.

[00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:39.400]
So the redundant 110 12 bits.

[00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:41.380]
They actually are used for error correction.

[00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:45.360]
And it takes 110.6 seconds technically.

[00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:49.380]
So it's quite a long transmission for sending 50 bits of data.

[00:12:50.140 --> 00:12:54.840]
The modulation is a frequency shift keying and what that means is essentially you're

[00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:57.640]
using different frequencies to indicate different symbols.

[00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:03.460]
And so essentially this transmission spectrum here, this graph, the y-axis is representing

[00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:04.980]
different frequencies being used.

[00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:06.740]
The x-axis is time.

[00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:12.220]
And you can see these different frequencies are encoding this message here or these tones

[00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:16.280]
here which is then further encoding the core sign location and power.

[00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:20.700]
And this is the actual message in data being sent in hex decimal.

[00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:23.160]
And total bandwidth is 6 hertz.

[00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:29.980]
So if you've ever done any radio stuff, you'd see that this is pretty unordinary in that.

[00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:34.360]
You're sending a very small amount of data over a very small bandwidth.

[00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:36.660]
